---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Plan Your Trip: Traditional Wear Guide | ChinaStyle"
  description="Plan your Chinese traditional clothing trip: visa tips, train routes, 5-14 day itineraries, budget breakdowns, best seasons, and hotel recommendations."
  canonicalUrl="/plan-trip"
  articleDate="2026-08-03"
  breadcrumbs={{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Plan Your Trip", url: "/plan-trip/" }, { name: "Plan Your Trip", url: "/plan-trip/" }}>
  <section class="pt-24 pb-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <span class="text-xs font-medium text-[#b5343a] tracking-[0.15em] uppercase">Travel Planning</span>
      <h1 class="font-serif-sc text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight text-stone-900 mt-3">
        Plan Your Traditional Wear Journey
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-stone-500 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        Everything you need to turn "I want to try Hanfu in China" into an actual trip with flights, trains, hotels, and booked experiences.
      </p>

      <!-- Quick jump nav -->
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <a href="#visa" class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-stone-100 rounded-full text-stone-600 hover:bg-stone-200 transition-colors">Visa & Entry</a>
        <a href="#getting-around" class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-stone-100 rounded-full text-stone-600 hover:bg-stone-200 transition-colors">Getting Around</a>
        <a href="#itineraries" class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-stone-100 rounded-full text-stone-600 hover:bg-stone-200 transition-colors">Itineraries</a>
        <a href="#budget" class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-stone-100 rounded-full text-stone-600 hover:bg-stone-200 transition-colors">Budget</a>
        <a href="#seasons" class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-stone-100 rounded-full text-stone-600 hover:bg-stone-200 transition-colors">When to Go</a>
        <a href="#packing" class="text-xs px-3 py-1.5 bg-stone-100 rounded-full text-stone-600 hover:bg-stone-200 transition-colors">What to Pack</a>
      </div>

      <!-- ===== VISA ===== -->
      <h2 id="visa" class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">Visa & Entry: Getting into China</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <strong>144-hour (6-day) Transit Visa-Free:</strong> Available in 27 Chinese cities including Beijing, Shanghai, Xi'an, Chengdu, Hangzhou, and Nanjing. You must arrive from one country and depart to a different country. Perfect for a focused 5-6 day trip covering 2 cities. Most nationalities qualify.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <strong>15-day Visa-Free (new!):</strong> As of 2024, citizens of France, Germany, Italy, Netherlands, Spain, Malaysia, Switzerland, Ireland, Hungary, Austria, Belgium, Luxembourg, Australia, New Zealand, Poland, and several others can enter China visa-free for up to 15 days. This is the easiest option — just show up with your passport.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <strong>Standard Tourist Visa (L Visa):</strong> If your country isn't on the visa-free list, apply at your nearest Chinese Visa Application Service Center (CVASC). Processing takes 4-7 business days. Required: passport (6+ months validity), completed form, passport photo, flight + hotel bookings, itinerary. Cost: approximately 30-150 USD depending on nationality and visa type.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 text-sm">
        <p class="text-stone-700"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Book refundable flights and hotels for your visa application, then adjust once approved. Always check the latest policy — China's visa rules change frequently and have been getting more relaxed.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- ===== GETTING AROUND ===== -->
      <h2 id="getting-around" class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">Getting Around China</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        China's high-speed rail network is the best way to travel between cities for a traditional wear tour. It's fast, punctual, and connects all the key destinations on our map.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-200 text-left">
              <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-stone-800">Route</th>
              <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-stone-800">Duration</th>
              <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-stone-800">Approx. Cost</th>
              <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-stone-800">Experience Link</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Beijing → Xi'an</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">4.5 - 5.5 hrs</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">515 - 825 RMB</td><td class="py-2.5 text-[#b5343a]">Tang suit + Hanfu</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Xi'an → Chengdu</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">3 - 4 hrs</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">263 - 420 RMB</td><td class="py-2.5 text-[#b5343a]">Hanfu + Tea culture</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Shanghai → Suzhou</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">25 - 30 min</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">40 - 70 RMB</td><td class="py-2.5 text-[#b5343a]">Qipao + Silk</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Shanghai → Hangzhou</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">45 - 60 min</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">73 - 117 RMB</td><td class="py-2.5 text-[#b5343a]">Silk + Song hanfu</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Hangzhou → Nanjing</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1.5 - 2 hrs</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">118 - 190 RMB</td><td class="py-2.5 text-[#b5343a]">Ming hanfu</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Guiyang → Kaili (Guizhou)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">40 min</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">59 - 95 RMB</td><td class="py-2.5 text-[#b5343a]">Miao embroidery</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Kunming → Dali</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">2 hrs</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">145 - 230 RMB</td><td class="py-2.5 text-[#b5343a]">Bai tie-dye</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-stone-400 mt-3">Book trains via <strong>trip.com</strong> or <strong>12306.cn</strong>. Book 1-2 weeks ahead for best availability. All stations have English signage on major routes.</p>

      <!-- ===== ITINERARIES ===== -->
      <h2 id="itineraries" class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">Sample Itineraries</h2>

      <div class="space-y-8">
        <!-- 7-day -->
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <span class="text-xs px-2.5 py-1 bg-[#b5343a] text-white rounded-full font-medium">7 Days</span>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-stone-900">Golden Triangle: Beijing → Xi'an</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600">
            <p><strong>Days 1-2 (Beijing):</strong> Arrive Beijing. Day 1: tang suit fitting in a hutong tailor shop (Nanluoguxiang). Day 2: Forbidden City tour, wear your tang suit for photos at Jingshan Park at sunset. Evening: Peking duck in Qianmen.</p>
            <p><strong>Days 3-4 (Beijing → Xi'an by train):</strong> Morning high-speed train (4.5 hrs). Afternoon: settle into hotel near city wall. Evening: walk the Muslim Quarter.</p>
            <p><strong>Days 5-6 (Xi'an):</strong> Day 5: Hanfu rental near South Gate, full photoshoot on city wall (budget 3-4 hrs). Evening: Datang Everbright City in full costume. Day 6: Terracotta Warriors (half-day trip), afternoon explore Daming Palace ruins.</p>
            <p><strong>Day 7 (Xi'an):</strong> Morning free for last photos or shopping. Afternoon departure.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 14-day -->
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <span class="text-xs px-2.5 py-1 bg-[#b5343a] text-white rounded-full font-medium">14 Days</span>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-stone-900">Silk Road of Style: Shanghai → Suzhou → Hangzhou → Nanjing → Xi'an</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600">
            <p><strong>Days 1-2 (Shanghai):</strong> Day 1: visit South Bund Fabric Market, get measured for a custom qipao. Day 2: French Concession walking tour in vintage style. Evening: Bund riverfront.</p>
            <p><strong>Day 3 (Suzhou, 30 min by train):</strong> Day trip. Morning: silk qipao rental, photos in Humble Administrator's Garden. Afternoon: Pingjiang Road for handcrafted fans and silk accessories. Return to Shanghai at night.</p>
            <p><strong>Days 4-5 (Hangzhou, 1 hr by train):</strong> Day 4: China National Silk Museum (free entry, costume try-on sessions). West Lake walk in Song dynasty hanfu. Day 5: tea plantation visit (Longjing Village), Hangzhou Silk City shopping.</p>
            <p><strong>Days 6-7 (Nanjing, 1.5 hrs by train):</strong> Day 6: Ming dynasty hanfu at Ming Xiaoling. Yunjin (silk brocade) museum. Day 7: Confucius Temple area, qipao shops, night market.</p>
            <p><strong>Days 8-10 (Xi'an, 5 hrs by train):</strong> Day 8: travel day, settle in. Day 9: full Hanfu day — Daming Palace morning, city wall sunset shoot. Day 10: Terracotta Warriors, Muslim Quarter.</p>
            <p><strong>Days 11-13 (Chengdu, 3.5 hrs by train):</strong> Day 11: travel + Jinli Ancient Street Hanfu stroll. Day 12: tea ceremony in traditional teahouse, Shu embroidery workshop. Day 13: panda base (morning), free afternoon.</p>
            <p><strong>Day 14:</strong> Departure from Chengdu.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 5-day -->
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <span class="text-xs px-2.5 py-1 bg-stone-500 text-white rounded-full font-medium">5 Days</span>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-stone-900">Quick Hanfu Fix: Xi'an Deep Dive</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600">
            <p><strong>Day 1:</strong> Arrive Xi'an. Evening: Bell Tower + Drum Tower lit up at night. Muslim Quarter street food.</p>
            <p><strong>Day 2:</strong> Morning: Hanfu rental fitting + hair/makeup (budget 1 hr). Full day in costume: city wall (3-4 hrs, rent a bike). Late afternoon: change dynasty style, head to Daming Palace for golden hour.</p>
            <p><strong>Day 3:</strong> Terracotta Warriors (half-day trip, arrange through hotel). Afternoon: Shaanxi History Museum. Evening: Tang Dynasty music and dance show.</p>
            <p><strong>Day 4:</strong> Second Hanfu day — try a different dynasty. Morning: Small Wild Goose Pagoda + Jianfu Temple. Afternoon: Qujiang Pool Park. Evening: Datang Everbright City in costume.</p>
            <p><strong>Day 5:</strong> Last morning shopping for silk souvenirs. Departure.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ===== BUDGET ===== -->
      <h2 id="budget" class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">Budget Breakdown</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">Per-person estimates for a 7-day Beijing-Xi'an trip (mid-range, not including international flights).</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead><tr class="border-b border-stone-200 text-left"><th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-stone-800">Item</th><th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-stone-800">Cost (RMB)</th><th class="py-3 font-semibold text-stone-800">Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Hotels (6 nights, mid-range)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1,800 - 3,600</td><td class="py-2.5">300-600 RMB/night</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">High-speed train (Beijing-Xi'an)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">515 - 825</td><td class="py-2.5">Second or first class</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Metro + taxi within cities</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">300 - 500</td><td class="py-2.5">Metro 3-9 RMB/ride</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Meals (7 days)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">700 - 1,400</td><td class="py-2.5">Street food to mid-range restaurants</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Hanfu rental + styling (2 sessions)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">300 - 800</td><td class="py-2.5">Basic to premium package</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Hanfu photoshoot (1 session w/ pro)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">500 - 1,500</td><td class="py-2.5">Optional but recommended</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Attraction tickets</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">400 - 700</td><td class="py-2.5">Wall, Forbidden City, Warriors, etc.</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Tang suit (custom)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">300 - 800</td><td class="py-2.5">Off-rack to custom silk</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 font-semibold">Total (excluding flights)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 font-semibold">4,800 - 10,100</td><td class="py-2.5">~660 - 1,390 USD</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <!-- ===== BEST SEASONS ===== -->
      <h2 id="seasons" class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">When to Go</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-lg">🌸</span> Spring (March-May)
          </h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-1.5 leading-relaxed"><strong>Best overall.</strong> Comfortable 15-25dC. Cherry blossoms in Xi'an, peonies in Luoyang, West Lake in Hangzhou at its most beautiful. Book ahead for May holidays (first week).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-lg">🍂</span> Autumn (September-November)
          </h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-1.5 leading-relaxed"><strong>Second best.</strong> Clear skies, golden light for photos. Xi'an city wall at sunset is spectacular. Avoid first week of October (National Day — extremely crowded everywhere).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-lg">❄️</span> Winter (December-February)
          </h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-1.5 leading-relaxed">Beijing can hit -10dC — tang suit under a coat works. Xi'an is milder. Fewer tourists = empty photos. Chinese New Year (late Jan/Feb): festive but everything closes for 3-5 days.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-lg">☀️</span> Summer (June-August)
          </h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-1.5 leading-relaxed">Hot and humid (30-38dC). Tang dynasty chest-high ruqun is lightweight and comfortable. Morning and evening photoshoots recommended. July-August is domestic school holiday — sites are busy.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ===== PACKING ===== -->
      <h2 id="packing" class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-16 mb-4">What to Pack</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm">Essential Apps</h4>
          <ul class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-2 space-y-1.5">
            <li>· <strong>Alipay / WeChat Pay</strong> — set up before arrival, link your card. Cash is rarely used.</li>
            <li>· <strong>Didi</strong> — Chinese Uber, available in English.</li>
            <li>· <strong>trip.com / 12306</strong> — train bookings.</li>
            <li>· <strong>Meituan</strong> — book Hanfu rentals at a discount.</li>
            <li>· <strong>Google Translate</strong> — download Chinese offline.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm">Clothing & Gear</h4>
          <ul class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-2 space-y-1.5">
            <li>· Comfortable walking shoes (you'll walk 15K+ steps/day at sites).</li>
            <li>· Slip-on shoes for Hanfu (easier to remove for costume changes).</li>
            <li>· Layers — spring/autumn mornings are cool, afternoons warm.</li>
            <li>· Portable charger (20,000 mAh) — you'll use your phone a lot.</li>
            <li>· VPN installed and tested before departure.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 text-sm">For the Photoshoot</h4>
          <ul class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-2 space-y-1.5">
            <li>· Nude undergarments (strapless bra, skin-colored shorts).</li>
            <li>· Minimal jewelry — Hanfu comes with its own accessories.</li>
            <li>· Light makeup base if you're sensitive to shared products.</li>
            <li>· Reference photos of looks you want (show the stylist).</li>
            <li>· A friend or selfie stick — or book a photographer.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
